  "comparison": "F I MAE=3.8056e-15 CCCS=0; F J MAE=4.5373e-13 CCCS=512; F S MAE=n/a CCCS=None. Exp 16 I has 0 CCCS and 48 dumps. Exp 17 A has 0 dumps and 48 TIA.",
  "failure_modes": "S computes a normalized current partition, not W2. J implements W2 but needs one current-copy gain per hidden-to-output weight (F: 512 CCCS). 0.1% copy error would return the Experiment 12/16 bottleneck at much larger count.",
  "dominant_error": "S: current-division identity I*G/G_tot. J: leftover CCCS copy gain plus output dump. I: leftover dump only.",
  "physical_interpretation": "Unit split matches G/G_tot (err=1.6941e-20) and mismatches w*I (err=6.0000e-06). Unit CCCS matches w*I (err=1.6941e-21). Kirchhoff current division is ratiometric and physically elegant, but it is not an unnormalized matrix multiply.",
  "hardware_level": "LEVEL 2 behavioral. J's CCCS would become MOS current mirrors with per-weight gain \u2014 not justified as a path that *reduces* active blocks.",
  "conclusion": "CURRENT-MODE CASCADE CANNOT REMOVE COPIES FOR FROZEN W2. A splitter is the wrong math. A CCCS fabric restores the copy explosion (512 on F). Hidden voltage conversion remains the cheaper way to apply an arbitrary next-layer weight matrix. J analog MAE=4.5373e-13 is fine and irrelevant: it loses on active-block count.",
  "next_question": "Given VG is required (Exp 18) and current cascade is not cheaper (Exp 20), what is the minimum active primitive of one neuron, and can finite TIA A be treated as a common-mode scale?",
  "plots": [
    "C:\\dev\\neuron-physical-brain\\physical-ai\\results\\current_cascade_mae.png",
    "C:\\dev\\neuron-physical-brain\\physical-ai\\results\\current_cascade_copies.png"
  ]
